<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1. </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">On a complete Riemannian manifold, the landmark differential gradient estimate of Li and
Yau controls positive solutions of the heat equation in terms of a lower Ricci-curvature
bound <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>]</cite>. In the case of nonnegative Ricci curvature, its integrated form gives a
parabolic Harnack inequality and, together with volume comparison, Gaussian heat-kernel
estimates. More generally, the work of Grigor’yan and Saloff-Coste shows that, for complete
Riemannian manifolds, the following three properties are quantitatively equivalent
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>, <a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">36</a>]</cite>:</p>
<ol id="S1.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(i)</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">volume doubling together with a scale-invariant <math id="S1.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>-Poincaré inequality;</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(ii)</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">a scale-invariant parabolic Harnack inequality;</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(iii)</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">two-sided Gaussian heat-kernel estimates.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
<p id="S1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">This equivalence makes volume doubling and Poincaré inequalities central geometric
counterparts of heat-flow regularity.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">There are two principal approaches to formulating lower Ricci bounds beyond the smooth
setting. The Bakry–Émery approach encodes curvature through the Bochner inequality and
<math id="S1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>-calculus for a Markov semigroup <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>, <a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite>. The approach of Lott–Villani
and Sturm instead uses displacement convexity of entropy along Wasserstein geodesics
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">28</a>, <a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">38</a>, <a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">39</a>]</cite>. On infinitesimally Hilbertian metric-measure spaces,
the corresponding Riemannian curvature-dimension and Bakry–Émery formulations are
equivalent <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>, <a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Both approaches have discrete analogues, but they lead to several inequivalent notions. For
graph Laplacians, the Bakry–Émery formalism leads to classical curvature-dimension
inequalities <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">26</a>, <a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite>, whereas transport ideas lead, among
other notions, to Ollivier’s coarse Ricci curvature and the entropic Ricci curvature of
Erbar and Maas <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">33</a>, <a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>]</cite>. There is no general identification among these
discrete curvatures. Moreover, a graph Laplacian is not a diffusion operator: the chain
rule used in the classical logarithmic Li–Yau argument fails. Within the Bakry–Émery
formalism, <math id="S1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{CD}(0,\infty)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>CD</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{CD}(0,\infty)</annotation></semantics></math> is the direct discrete version of the nonnegative
Bochner inequality, yet it was not known whether, on bounded-degree simple graphs, this
condition alone forces volume doubling or a scale-invariant ball Poincaré inequality
with constants depending only on the degree bound.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">In this paper we resolve both questions. Our argument builds on the modified nonlinear
heat equation introduced by Münch for finite graphs and extended by Pajot and Russ to
infinite bounded-geometry graphs <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">31</a>, <a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">34</a>]</cite>. We prove that classical
nonnegative Bakry–Émery curvature controls the large-scale geometry of every connected
simple graph of bounded degree: it implies uniform volume doubling, polynomial volume
growth, and a scale-invariant local <math id="S1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>-Poincaré inequality. The curvature hypothesis is
the dimension-free quadratic condition <math id="S1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{CD}(0,\infty)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>CD</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{CD}(0,\infty)</annotation></semantics></math> for the unnormalised
Laplacian; no finite-dimensional curvature condition, exponential curvature-dimension
condition, regularity, or edge-regularity is assumed. The new dimension-free input consists
of point-mass consequences of the positive semidefinite form <math id="S1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma_{2}(\cdot,\cdot)(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma_{2}(\cdot,\cdot)(x)</annotation></semantics></math>
and a positive-resolvent smoothing estimate. The Poincaré inequality additionally requires
a heat-kernel localisation argument.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Throughout the paper, <math id="S1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G=(V,E)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G=(V,E)</annotation></semantics></math> denotes a connected simple undirected graph. We work with
the unnormalised graph Laplacian</p>
<table id="S1.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta f(x)=\sum_{y\sim x}\bigl(f(y)-f(x)\bigr)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta f(x)=\sum_{y\sim x}\bigl(f(y)-f(x)\bigr).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.p5.2" class="ltx_p">Its carré du champ and iterated carré du champ are denoted simply by</p>
<table id="S1.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma(f,g)=\frac{1}{2}\bigl(\Delta(fg)-f\Delta g-g\Delta f\bigr)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma(f,g)=\frac{1}{2}\bigl(\Delta(fg)-f\Delta g-g\Delta f\bigr)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.p5.3" class="ltx_p">and</p>
<table id="S1.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma_{2}(f,g)=\frac{1}{2}\bigl(\Delta\Gamma(f,g)-\Gamma(f,\Delta g)-\Gamma(g,\Delta f)\bigr)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma_{2}(f,g)=\frac{1}{2}\bigl(\Delta\Gamma(f,g)-\Gamma(f,\Delta g)-\Gamma(g,\Delta f)\bigr).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.p5.4" class="ltx_p">We write <math id="S1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma(f)=\Gamma(f,f)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma(f)=\Gamma(f,f)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma_{2}(f)=\Gamma_{2}(f,f)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma_{2}(f)=\Gamma_{2}(f,f)</annotation></semantics></math>. The following two
assumptions will be used throughout.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmassumption1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_assumption">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmassumption1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Assumption A</span></span><span id="Thmassumption1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmassumption1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmassumption1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmassumption1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The graph has bounded degree:</span></p>
<table id="S1.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{*}:=\sup_{x\in V}\deg(x)&lt;\infty." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo rspace="0.1389em">:=</mo><mrow><munder><mo lspace="0.1389em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">sup</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><mi>deg</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{*}:=\sup_{x\in V}\deg(x)&lt;\infty.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmassumption2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_assumption">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmassumption2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Assumption B</span></span><span id="Thmassumption2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmassumption2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmassumption2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmassumption2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The unnormalised Laplacian satisfies the classical condition <math id="Thmassumption2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{CD}(0,\infty)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>CD</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{CD}(0,\infty)</annotation></semantics></math>:</span></p>
<table id="S1.Ex5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma_{2}(f)(x)\geq 0" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma_{2}(f)(x)\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmassumption2.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmassumption2.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">for every <math id="Thmassumption2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in V</annotation></semantics></math> and every real-valued function <math id="Thmassumption2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="Thmassumption2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Assumption <a href="#Thmassumption1" title="Assumption A. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Nonnegative Bakry–Émery Curvature on Bounded-Degree Graphs Implies Volume Doubling and Poincaré Inequalities" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a> implies local finiteness. Moreover,
<math id="S1.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma_{2}(f)(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma_{2}(f)(x)</annotation></semantics></math> depends only on the values of <math id="S1.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> in the two-ball about <math id="S1.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>, so
Assumption <a href="#Thmassumption2" title="Assumption B. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Nonnegative Bakry–Émery Curvature on Bounded-Degree Graphs Implies Volume Doubling and Poincaré Inequalities" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a> is meaningful on infinite graphs and is unchanged if one tests
only finitely supported functions. We use open graph balls</p>
<table id="S1.Ex6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B(x,r):=\{y\in V:\operatorname{dist}(x,y)&lt;r\}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>dist</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B(x,r):=\{y\in V:\operatorname{dist}(x,y)&lt;r\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.p6.2" class="ltx_p">For integer radii this convention differs from the closed-ball convention used in parts of the
literature only by shifting the radius by one. All volume-doubling and ball-Poincaré statements
below are formulated at positive integer radii, the natural graph scales.</p>
</div>
<section id="S1.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">1.1. </span>Curvature conditions and discrete Li–Yau theories</h3>

<div id="S1.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For graph Laplacians, the classical carré-du-champ theory was developed by Lin and Yau
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">26</a>]</cite>, Bauer, Horn, Lin, Lippner, Mangoubi, and Yau <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite>, and many subsequent
authors, building on the diffusion formalism of Bakry and Èmery <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>, <a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite>. The local
curvature-function and matrix viewpoint was developed systematically by Cushing, Liu, and
Peyerimhoff <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite>. These formulations make the local nature of graph Bakry–Émery
curvature explicit, but they do not restore the diffusion chain rule.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Several curvature conditions with similar notation must be kept separate. For <math id="S1.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n&lt;\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n&lt;\infty</annotation></semantics></math>, the
classical condition is</p>
<table id="S1.Ex7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{CD}(K,n):\qquad\Gamma_{2}(f)\geq K\Gamma(f)+\frac{1}{n}(\Delta f)^{2}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>CD</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><mspace style="width:2em;" width="2em"></mspace><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{CD}(K,n):\qquad\Gamma_{2}(f)\geq K\Gamma(f)+\frac{1}{n}(\Delta f)^{2}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.SS1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">Thus <math id="S1.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{CD}(K,n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>CD</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{CD}(K,n)</annotation></semantics></math> implies <math id="S1.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{CD}(K,\infty)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>CD</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{CD}(K,\infty)</annotation></semantics></math>, and the latter contains no finite
parameter. A graph Laplacian is not a diffusion operator, however, so the chain rule needed in
the manifold proof of the logarithmic Li–Yau estimate fails. This led to several nonlinear
substitutes.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Bauer et al. introduced the exponential curvature-dimension framework
<math id="S1.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{CDE}(n,K)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>CDE</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{CDE}(n,K)</annotation></semantics></math> and proved Li–Yau, Harnack, heat-kernel, Buser, and volume-growth
consequences <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite>. Horn, Lin, Liu, and Yau later worked under the stronger
condition <math id="S1.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{CDE}^{\prime}(n,K)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>CDE</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{CDE}^{\prime}(n,K)</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>]</cite>; Münch proved that
<math id="S1.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{CDE}^{\prime}(n,K)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>CDE</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{CDE}^{\prime}(n,K)</annotation></semantics></math> implies the classical condition <math id="S1.SS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{CD}(K,n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>CD</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{CD}(K,n)</annotation></semantics></math>
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">29</a>]</cite>. Hence <math id="S1.SS1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{CDE}^{\prime}(n,0)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>CDE</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{CDE}^{\prime}(n,0)</annotation></semantics></math> is formally stronger than the hypothesis of
the present paper both in curvature type and in dimension. By contrast,
<math id="S1.SS1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{CDE}(n,0)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>CDE</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{CDE}(n,0)</annotation></semantics></math> is a distinct finite-dimensional nonlinear condition, and no general
implication from the classical condition used here to <math id="S1.SS1.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{CDE}(n,0)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>CDE</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{CDE}(n,0)</annotation></semantics></math> is known.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">A different nonlinear approach was developed by Münch using a modified carré-du-champ
calculus <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">30</a>]</cite>. Dier, Kassmann, and Zacher introduced a
<math id="S1.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{CD}(F;0)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>CD</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>F</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{CD}(F;0)</annotation></semantics></math> condition and obtained discrete logarithmic Li–Yau and differential Harnack
estimates for positive solutions of the standard heat equation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite>. Their condition is
again distinct: its general relationship with the classical <math id="S1.SS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{CD}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>CD</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{CD}</annotation></semantics></math> and exponential
<math id="S1.SS1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{CDE}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>CDE</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{CDE}</annotation></semantics></math> conditions is not known. These works are important predecessors for the
heat-flow viewpoint, but they do not by themselves give the volume-doubling and metric-ball
Poincaré statements proved below under classical <math id="S1.SS1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{CD}(0,\infty)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>CD</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{CD}(0,\infty)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">For the classical condition itself, Lin and Liu identified the bounded-generator equivalence with
heat-semigroup gradient and variance inequalities <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">25</a>]</cite>; Keller and Münch obtained
related semigroup characterisations for unbounded graph Laplacians under suitable domain and
ellipticity hypotheses <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>]</cite>. In particular, <math id="S1.SS1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{CD}(0,\infty)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>CD</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{CD}(0,\infty)</annotation></semantics></math> yields the
pointwise semigroup estimate often called a “reverse Poincaré inequality.” This terminology
should not be confused with the local scale-invariant ball Poincaré inequality in
Theorem <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem3" title="Theorem 1.3. ‣ 1.3. The local Poincaré problem ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Nonnegative Bakry–Émery Curvature on Bounded-Degree Graphs Implies Volume Doubling and Poincaré Inequalities" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.3</span></a> below. The same warning applies to pseudo-Poincaré estimates
and to global Poincaré, spectral-gap, or Buser inequalities
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">27</a>, <a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>, <a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>]</cite>: none of these is, without an additional localisation
argument, a uniform Neumann-type inequality on every metric ball.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S1.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">1.2. </span>The volume-growth problem and its history</h3>

<div id="S1.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The expectation that nonnegative discrete curvature should force polynomial volume growth can
be traced at least implicitly to Bauer et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite>. Under
<math id="S1.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{CDE}(n,0)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>CDE</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{CDE}(n,0)</annotation></semantics></math> they proved that bounded-degree graphs have polynomial volume growth
(in fact an upper bound of order <math id="S1.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r^{2n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>r</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r^{2n}</annotation></semantics></math> in their normalisation), together with Li–Yau and
standard-heat Harnack estimates and one-sided heat-kernel bounds. This was the first general
theorem of the form “nonnegative curvature implies polynomial growth” in the discrete
Bakry–Émery setting, and it is the natural historical source of the later folklore conjecture.
The general <math id="S1.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{CDE}(n,0)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>CDE</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{CDE}(n,0)</annotation></semantics></math> argument did not, however, yield scale-uniform volume doubling
or the metric-ball Poincaré inequality; the usual local package was recovered there under
additional strong cut-off hypotheses.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Horn, Lin, Liu, and Yau obtained a sharper analytic package under
<math id="S1.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{CDE}^{\prime}(n,0)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>CDE</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{CDE}^{\prime}(n,0)</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>]</cite>. Their semigroup argument proves volume doubling under
<math id="S1.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{CDE}^{\prime}(n,0)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>CDE</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{CDE}^{\prime}(n,0)</annotation></semantics></math>; after the usual <math id="S1.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta(\alpha)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta(\alpha)</annotation></semantics></math> ellipticity and laziness condition is
imposed, they obtain two-sided discrete-time Gaussian estimates, the weak scale-invariant
Poincaré inequality, and the parabolic Harnack inequality through Delmotte’s theorem
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite>. The logical distinction is important: by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">29</a>]</cite>, their
hypothesis implies a finite-dimensional classical condition, whereas Assumption <a href="#Thmassumption2" title="Assumption B. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Nonnegative Bakry–Émery Curvature on Bounded-Degree Graphs Implies Volume Doubling and Poincaré Inequalities" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a>
is dimension-free.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The bounded-degree conjecture for the <em id="S1.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">classical</em> condition was formulated explicitly by
Cushing, Liu, and Peyerimhoff as a discrete Bishop-type problem
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>, Conjecture 9.10]</cite>. They asked whether there are constants depending only on the
degree bound such that</p>
<table id="S1.Ex8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\#B(x,r)\leq C_{1}(d_{*})\bigl(1+r^{C_{2}(d_{*})}\bigr)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>#</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>r</mi><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\#B(x,r)\leq C_{1}(d_{*})\bigl(1+r^{C_{2}(d_{*})}\bigr)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.SS2.p3.2" class="ltx_p">for every graph satisfying Assumptions <a href="#Thmassumption1" title="Assumption A. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Nonnegative Bakry–Émery Curvature on Bounded-Degree Graphs Implies Volume Doubling and Poincaré Inequalities" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a> and <a href="#Thmassumption2" title="Assumption B. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Nonnegative Bakry–Émery Curvature on Bounded-Degree Graphs Implies Volume Doubling and Poincaré Inequalities" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a>. Their work
also developed the local curvature-function and matrix viewpoint and exhibited finite-dimensional
obstructions. Salez later proved that bounded-degree expander families cannot satisfy
<math id="S1.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{CD}(0,\infty)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>CD</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{CD}(0,\infty)</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">35</a>]</cite>, settling a necessary consequence of the conjecture but
not controlling volume growth or providing a scale-by-scale volume comparison.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Classical finite dimension was understood more recently. Münch introduced the modified heat
equation</p>
<table id="S1.Ex9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial_{t}u=\Delta u+\Gamma(u)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∂</mo><mi>t</mi></msub><mi>u</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial_{t}u=\Delta u+\Gamma(u)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.SS2.p4.2" class="ltx_p">and proved volume doubling for finite graphs satisfying <math id="S1.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{CD}(0,n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>CD</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{CD}(0,n)</annotation></semantics></math>
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">31</a>]</cite>. Pajot and Russ extended the construction and the doubling theorem to finite
or infinite weighted graphs of bounded geometry <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">34</a>]</cite>. Their Harnack inequality is
for this nonlinear modified heat equation; it is therefore not a standard heat-kernel Harnack
inequality and does not directly yield the metric-ball Poincaré inequality through Delmotte’s
equivalence.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Blachar, Pajot, and Salez recently reformulated the classical dimension-free conjecture in the
integer-radius form stated below and proved the stronger volume-doubling conclusion for
edge-regular graphs <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite>. Their key input is an optimal finite-dimensional
self-improvement depending on the degree and the common-neighbour parameter. This includes
many symmetric examples but not arbitrary bounded-degree graphs, so the general bounded-degree
case remained open.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS2.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS2.p6.1" class="ltx_p">The bounded-geometry hypothesis cannot simply be omitted or replaced by an arbitrary
normalised-Laplacian statement. The exponentially growing normalised antitrees in
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite> have nonnegative (indeed positive pointwise) normalised Bakry–Émery
curvature while failing polynomial growth and volume doubling; their degrees are unbounded.
This is why the physical, unnormalised Laplacian and the uniform degree bound in
Assumptions <a href="#Thmassumption1" title="Assumption A. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Nonnegative Bakry–Émery Curvature on Bounded-Degree Graphs Implies Volume Doubling and Poincaré Inequalities" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a>–<a href="#Thmassumption2" title="Assumption B. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Nonnegative Bakry–Émery Curvature on Bounded-Degree Graphs Implies Volume Doubling and Poincaré Inequalities" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a> are substantive.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS2.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS2.p7.1" class="ltx_p">With our open-ball convention, the recent integer-radius formulation is as follows.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmconjecture1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_conjecture">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmconjecture1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Conjecture 1</span></span><span id="Thmconjecture1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmconjecture1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmconjecture1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmconjecture1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Suppose Assumptions <a href="#Thmassumption1" title="Assumption A. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Nonnegative Bakry–Émery Curvature on Bounded-Degree Graphs Implies Volume Doubling and Poincaré Inequalities" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a> and <a href="#Thmassumption2" title="Assumption B. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Nonnegative Bakry–Émery Curvature on Bounded-Degree Graphs Implies Volume Doubling and Poincaré Inequalities" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a> hold. Then there is a finite
exponent <math id="Thmconjecture1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D(d_{*})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D(d_{*})</annotation></semantics></math> such that</span></p>
<table id="S1.Ex10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\#B(x,r)\leq r^{D(d_{*})}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>#</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><msup><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\#B(x,r)\leq r^{D(d_{*})}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmconjecture1.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmconjecture1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">for every <math id="Thmconjecture1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in V</annotation></semantics></math> and every integer <math id="Thmconjecture1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS2.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS2.p8.1" class="ltx_p">The formulation with a multiplicative constant in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite> and the normalised
integer-radius formulation in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite> are equivalent after changing the exponent by an
amount depending only on <math id="S1.SS2.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{*}</annotation></semantics></math>. Conjecture <a href="#Thmconjecture1" title="Conjecture 1. ‣ 1.2. The volume-growth problem and its history ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Nonnegative Bakry–Émery Curvature on Bounded-Degree Graphs Implies Volume Doubling and Poincaré Inequalities" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> asks whether a local,
infinite-dimensional quadratic inequality imposes a uniform finite-dimensional geometry at all
scales. It asks for substantially more than the absence of expanders, and its difficulty is
specifically discrete: the standard manifold proof uses both a finite dimension and a diffusion
chain rule, neither of which is available under Assumption <a href="#Thmassumption2" title="Assumption B. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Nonnegative Bakry–Émery Curvature on Bounded-Degree Graphs Implies Volume Doubling and Poincaré Inequalities" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS2.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS2.p9.1" class="ltx_p">Our first main result proves the conjecture in the stronger doubling form.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 1.1</span></span><span id="S1.Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Suppose Assumptions <a href="#Thmassumption1" title="Assumption A. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Nonnegative Bakry–Émery Curvature on Bounded-Degree Graphs Implies Volume Doubling and Poincaré Inequalities" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a> and <a href="#Thmassumption2" title="Assumption B. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Nonnegative Bakry–Émery Curvature on Bounded-Degree Graphs Implies Volume Doubling and Poincaré Inequalities" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a> hold. Then there is a finite
constant <math id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K(d_{*})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K(d_{*})</annotation></semantics></math> such that</span></p>
<table id="S1.Ex11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\#B(x,2r)\leq K(d_{*})\,\#B(x,r)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>#</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>#</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\#B(x,2r)\leq K(d_{*})\,\#B(x,r)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">for every <math id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in V</annotation></semantics></math> and every integer <math id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS2.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS2.p10.1" class="ltx_p">Theorem <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 1.1. ‣ 1.2. The volume-growth problem and its history ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Nonnegative Bakry–Émery Curvature on Bounded-Degree Graphs Implies Volume Doubling and Poincaré Inequalities" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.1</span></a> is stronger at the conclusion level than the polynomial upper bound
proved under <math id="S1.SS2.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{CDE}(n,0)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>CDE</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{CDE}(n,0)</annotation></semantics></math> in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite> and than the conclusions conjectured in
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>, <a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite>: doubling controls the ratio of two consecutive scales and therefore supplies
both upper and lower interscale information. At the assumption level it removes the finite
classical dimension required in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">31</a>, <a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">34</a>]</cite>, the
<math id="S1.SS2.p10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{CDE}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>CDE</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{CDE}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> hypothesis used in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>]</cite>, and the edge-regularity used in
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite>. Relative to <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite>, the curvature conditions are different and no formal
implication is asserted; what is removed here is their finite-dimensional exponential
curvature hypothesis.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS2.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS2.p11.1" class="ltx_p">Iterating the estimate at dyadic radii gives the conjectured polynomial bound.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_corollary">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary 1.2</span></span><span id="S1.Thmtheorem2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Suppose Assumptions <a href="#Thmassumption1" title="Assumption A. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Nonnegative Bakry–Émery Curvature on Bounded-Degree Graphs Implies Volume Doubling and Poincaré Inequalities" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a> and <a href="#Thmassumption2" title="Assumption B. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Nonnegative Bakry–Émery Curvature on Bounded-Degree Graphs Implies Volume Doubling and Poincaré Inequalities" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a> hold. Then</span></p>
<table id="S1.Ex12" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\#B(x,r)\leq r^{D(d_{*})}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>#</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><msup><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\#B(x,r)\leq r^{D(d_{*})}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.Thmtheorem2.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem2.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">for every <math id="S1.Thmtheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in V</annotation></semantics></math> and every integer <math id="S1.Thmtheorem2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math>, where one may take</span></p>
<table id="S1.Ex13" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D(d_{*}):=\left\lceil 2\log_{2}K(d_{*})\right\rceil." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo>⌈</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msub><mi>log</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>⌉</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D(d_{*}):=\left\lceil 2\log_{2}K(d_{*})\right\rceil.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S1.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">1.3. </span>The local Poincaré problem</h3>

<div id="S1.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For a nonempty finite set <math id="S1.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D\subset V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D\subset V</annotation></semantics></math>, define</p>
<table id="S1.Ex14" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{D}:=\frac{1}{\#D}\sum_{x\in D}f(x)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>D</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi>#</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{D}:=\frac{1}{\#D}\sum_{x\in D}f(x)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.SS3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">and</p>
<table id="S1.Ex15" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}_{D}(f):=\sum_{\begin{subarray}{c}\{x,y\}\in E\\
x,y\in D\end{subarray}}\bigl(f(x)-f(y)\bigr)^{2}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>D</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">:=</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mtable rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></munder><msup><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}_{D}(f):=\sum_{\begin{subarray}{c}\{x,y\}\in E\\
x,y\in D\end{subarray}}\bigl(f(x)-f(y)\bigr)^{2}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.SS3.p1.3" class="ltx_p">The weak scale-invariant ball Poincaré inequality asks for constants <math id="S1.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> and a fixed dilation
<math id="S1.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> such that</p>
<table id="S1.Ex16" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{z\in B(x,r)}|f(z)-f_{B(x,r)}|^{2}\leq Cr^{2}\mathcal{E}_{B(x,\lambda r)}(f)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></munder><msup><mrow><mo lspace="0em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>r</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{z\in B(x,r)}|f(z)-f_{B(x,r)}|^{2}\leq Cr^{2}\mathcal{E}_{B(x,\lambda r)}(f)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.SS3.p1.4" class="ltx_p">uniformly in <math id="S1.SS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>, positive integer <math id="S1.SS3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S1.SS3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math>. The word “weak” refers only to the enlarged
ball on the right-hand side.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">On uniformly elliptic lazy reversible graphs, Delmotte proved the quantitative equivalence
between volume doubling plus this ball Poincaré inequality, parabolic Harnack inequalities,
and two-sided discrete-time Gaussian heat-kernel estimates <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite>. In particular, the
ball Poincaré inequality is an independent localisation requirement; it is not a formal
consequence of volume doubling. The finite-dimensional modified-heat results
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">31</a>, <a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">34</a>]</cite> provide the volume input but, prior to the present work, did not give
a general ball Poincaré theorem for classical <math id="S1.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{CD}(0,n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>CD</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{CD}(0,n)</annotation></semantics></math>. Nor do the pointwise
semigroup reverse Poincaré estimates of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">25</a>, <a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>]</cite>, the pseudo-Poincaré
estimate in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">27</a>]</cite>, the global Buser inequalities in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>, <a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">27</a>]</cite>, or the
conical-curvature global inequality in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>]</cite> furnish the required localisation
on arbitrary balls.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The strongest previous general positive theorem is the result of Horn, Lin, Liu, and Yau:
<math id="S1.SS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{CDE}^{\prime}(n,0)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>CDE</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{CDE}^{\prime}(n,0)</annotation></semantics></math> gives doubling, and together with <math id="S1.SS3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta(\alpha)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta(\alpha)</annotation></semantics></math> it gives the ball
Poincaré inequality, Gaussian estimates, and parabolic Harnack inequalities
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>]</cite>. The present theorem reaches the ball Poincaré conclusion from the classical
dimension-free curvature condition together with the standing maximum-degree bound.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 1.3</span></span><span id="S1.Thmtheorem3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmtheorem3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Suppose Assumptions <a href="#Thmassumption1" title="Assumption A. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Nonnegative Bakry–Émery Curvature on Bounded-Degree Graphs Implies Volume Doubling and Poincaré Inequalities" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a> and <a href="#Thmassumption2" title="Assumption B. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Nonnegative Bakry–Émery Curvature on Bounded-Degree Graphs Implies Volume Doubling and Poincaré Inequalities" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a> hold. Then there is a finite
constant <math id="S1.Thmtheorem3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{P}(d_{*})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>P</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{P}(d_{*})</annotation></semantics></math> such that</span></p>
<table id="S1.Ex17" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{z\in B(x,r)}\left|f(z)-f_{B(x,r)}\right|^{2}\leq C_{P}(d_{*})\,r^{2}\,\mathcal{E}_{B(x,2r)}(f)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></munder><msup><mrow><mo lspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>|</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>P</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>r</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{z\in B(x,r)}\left|f(z)-f_{B(x,r)}\right|^{2}\leq C_{P}(d_{*})\,r^{2}\,\mathcal{E}_{B(x,2r)}(f)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.Thmtheorem3.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem3.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">for every <math id="S1.Thmtheorem3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in V</annotation></semantics></math>, every integer <math id="S1.Thmtheorem3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math>, and every function <math id="S1.Thmtheorem3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:V\to\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:V\to\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">The closest results and the precise assumption–conclusion comparison are summarised in
Table <a href="#S1.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 1.3. The local Poincaré problem ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Nonnegative Bakry–Émery Curvature on Bounded-Degree Graphs Implies Volume Doubling and Poincaré Inequalities" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>. The table separates curvature hypotheses that are sometimes
conflated in the literature.</p>
</div>
