<div id="Thmremarkx1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmremarkx1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Remark</span></span><span id="Thmremarkx1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmremarkx1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmremarkx1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmremarkx1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The case <math id="Thmremarkx1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{*}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{*}=0</annotation></semantics></math> is trivial. If <math id="Thmremarkx1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{*}\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{*}\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math>, consider the lazy discrete-time kernel</span></p>
<table id="S1.Ex18" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{Q}:=I+\frac{\Delta}{2d_{*}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>Q</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{Q}:=I+\frac{\Delta}{2d_{*}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmremarkx1.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmremarkx1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Every edge transition has probability <math id="Thmremarkx1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1/(2d_{*})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1/(2d_{*})</annotation></semantics></math>, and every holding probability is at least
<math id="Thmremarkx1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1/2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1/2</annotation></semantics></math>. Its Dirichlet form is <math id="Thmremarkx1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(2d_{*})^{-1}\mathcal{E}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(2d_{*})^{-1}\mathcal{E}</annotation></semantics></math>, so
Theorems <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 1.1. ‣ 1.2. The volume-growth problem and its history ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Nonnegative Bakry–Émery Curvature on Bounded-Degree Graphs Implies Volume Doubling and Poincaré Inequalities" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.1</span></a> and <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem3" title="Theorem 1.3. ‣ 1.3. The local Poincaré problem ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Nonnegative Bakry–Émery Curvature on Bounded-Degree Graphs Implies Volume Doubling and Poincaré Inequalities" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.3</span></a> provide Delmotte’s volume-doubling and
weak Poincaré hypotheses with constants depending only on <math id="Thmremarkx1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{*}</annotation></semantics></math>. Delmotte’s equivalence
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite> therefore yields the corresponding two-sided discrete-time Gaussian heat-kernel
estimates and standard parabolic Harnack inequality. Thus, under the standing bounded-degree
framework, the conclusion-level package of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>]</cite> is recovered from the weaker classical
dimension-free curvature hypothesis.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S1.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">1.4. </span>Why the proof does not use finite-dimensional self-improvement</h3>

<div id="S1.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A natural route to Corollary <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem2" title="Corollary 1.2. ‣ 1.2. The volume-growth problem and its history ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Nonnegative Bakry–Émery Curvature on Bounded-Degree Graphs Implies Volume Doubling and Poincaré Inequalities" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.2</span></a> would be a local finite-dimensional
self-improvement. At a vertex <math id="S1.SS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>, restrict functions to the finite two-ball and set</p>
<table id="S1.Ex19" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{x}(f,g):=\Gamma_{2}(f,g)(x),\qquad\ell_{x}(f):=\Delta f(x)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{x}(f,g):=\Gamma_{2}(f,g)(x),\qquad\ell_{x}(f):=\Delta f(x).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.SS4.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Under Assumption <a href="#Thmassumption2" title="Assumption B. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Nonnegative Bakry–Émery Curvature on Bounded-Degree Graphs Implies Volume Doubling and Poincaré Inequalities" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a>, the form <math id="S1.SS4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{x}</annotation></semantics></math> is positive semidefinite. A finite local
dimension <math id="S1.SS4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{x}</annotation></semantics></math> with</p>
<table id="S1.Ex20" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex20.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{x}(f,f)\geq\frac{\ell_{x}(f)^{2}}{n_{x}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>x</mi></msub></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{x}(f,f)\geq\frac{\ell_{x}(f)^{2}}{n_{x}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.SS4.p1.3" class="ltx_p">exists precisely when</p>
<table id="S1.Ex21" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex21.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ker Q_{x}\subseteq\ker\ell_{x};" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>ker</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>x</mi></msub></mrow><mo>⊆</mo><mrow><mi>ker</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>x</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>;</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ker Q_{x}\subseteq\ker\ell_{x};</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.SS4.p1.4" class="ltx_p">in that case the optimal value is</p>
<table id="S1.Ex22" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex22.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{x}^{\mathrm{opt}}=\sup_{Q_{x}(f,f)&gt;0}\frac{\ell_{x}(f)^{2}}{Q_{x}(f,f)}&lt;\infty." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>n</mi><mi>x</mi><mi>opt</mi></msubsup><mo rspace="0.1389em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo lspace="0.1389em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">sup</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></munder><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{x}^{\mathrm{opt}}=\sup_{Q_{x}(f,f)&gt;0}\frac{\ell_{x}(f)^{2}}{Q_{x}(f,f)}&lt;\infty.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.SS4.p1.5" class="ltx_p">The edge-regular calculation of Blachar, Pajot, and Salez proves this kernel inclusion with an
optimal dimension depending on the degree and common-neighbour parameter, and they
conjectured that every <math id="S1.SS4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>-regular <math id="S1.SS4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{CD}(0,\infty)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>CD</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{CD}(0,\infty)</annotation></semantics></math> graph satisfies
<math id="S1.SS4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{CD}(0,d)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>CD</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{CD}(0,d)</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>, Conjecture 2]</cite>.<span id="footnote1" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">1</span>
              
              
              
            In a private preprint (available upon request), we disprove Conjecture 2 of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite>: for each <math id="footnote1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d\geq 5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d\geq 5</annotation></semantics></math> there is a
finite connected <math id="footnote1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>-regular <math id="footnote1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{CD}(0,\infty)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>CD</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{CD}(0,\infty)</annotation></semantics></math> graph satisfying no
<math id="footnote1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{CD}(0,n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>CD</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{CD}(0,n)</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="footnote1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n&lt;\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n&lt;\infty</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></span></span> The available finite-dimensional
self-improvement therefore requires additional local structure and is not available under the
standing bounded-degree assumptions. Accordingly, the present proof does not proceed through
a global finite-dimensional reduction.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Instead, we extract only the two consequences of the positive semidefinite form <math id="S1.SS4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{x}</annotation></semantics></math> that are
needed by the modified-heat argument. Testing <math id="S1.SS4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{x}</annotation></semantics></math> against the point mass at <math id="S1.SS4.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> gives an
effective dimension estimate at a negative local minimum of the Laplacian. Factoring the same
coercive expression through a positive resolvent gives an <math id="S1.SS4.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L^{\infty}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>L</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L^{\infty}</annotation></semantics></math> estimate for <math id="S1.SS4.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta P_{t}f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta P_{t}f</annotation></semantics></math>. The
first estimate enters a modified Li–Yau maximum principle; the second controls semigroup
displacement. Together with two exponential comparisons for the modified heat flow, they yield
a Harnack inequality and then volume doubling.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS4.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The additional difficulty in Theorem <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem3" title="Theorem 1.3. ‣ 1.3. The local Poincaré problem ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Nonnegative Bakry–Émery Curvature on Bounded-Degree Graphs Implies Volume Doubling and Poincaré Inequalities" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.3</span></a> is localisation. The standard heat
kernel has global support, whereas the desired right-hand side contains only the energy in
<math id="S1.SS4.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B(x,2r)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B(x,2r)</annotation></semantics></math>. The classical reverse Poincaré estimate gives overlap of nearby heat-kernel rows,
the resolvent estimate gives a diffusive first-moment displacement bound, and a strong-Markov
argument converts this into exit-time control. Coupling with the finite-state chain obtained by
rejecting boundary-crossing jumps, followed by a finite-dimensional kernel-localisation lemma,
then yields a scale-invariant Poincaré inequality with a fixed degree-dependent dilation.
Theorem <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 1.1. ‣ 1.2. The volume-growth problem and its history ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Nonnegative Bakry–Émery Curvature on Bounded-Degree Graphs Implies Volume Doubling and Poincaré Inequalities" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.1</span></a> is used only to reduce that dilation to two.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS4.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS4.p4.1" class="ltx_p">The paper is organised as follows. Section <a href="#S2" title="2. Preliminary ‣ Nonnegative Bakry–Émery Curvature on Bounded-Degree Graphs Implies Volume Doubling and Poincaré Inequalities" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> records the heat
semigroup generated directly by <math id="S1.SS4.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math> and the basic <math id="S1.SS4.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math>-calculus. Section <a href="#S3" title="3. Point-mass estimates and resolvent smoothing ‣ Nonnegative Bakry–Émery Curvature on Bounded-Degree Graphs Implies Volume Doubling and Poincaré Inequalities" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> proves the
point-mass and resolvent estimates. Section <a href="#S4" title="4. Modified heat, Li–Yau, and Harnack estimates ‣ Nonnegative Bakry–Émery Curvature on Bounded-Degree Graphs Implies Volume Doubling and Poincaré Inequalities" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> develops the modified heat
equation and derives the Li–Yau and Harnack inequalities. Section <a href="#S5" title="5. Volume doubling and polynomial growth ‣ Nonnegative Bakry–Émery Curvature on Bounded-Degree Graphs Implies Volume Doubling and Poincaré Inequalities" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>
proves Theorem <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 1.1. ‣ 1.2. The volume-growth problem and its history ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Nonnegative Bakry–Émery Curvature on Bounded-Degree Graphs Implies Volume Doubling and Poincaré Inequalities" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.1</span></a> and Corollary <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem2" title="Corollary 1.2. ‣ 1.2. The volume-growth problem and its history ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Nonnegative Bakry–Émery Curvature on Bounded-Degree Graphs Implies Volume Doubling and Poincaré Inequalities" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.2</span></a>.
Section <a href="#S6" title="6. Heat-kernel localisation for Poincaré inequalities ‣ Nonnegative Bakry–Émery Curvature on Bounded-Degree Graphs Implies Volume Doubling and Poincaré Inequalities" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a> establishes heat-kernel localisation and the
fixed-dilation Poincaré inequality, and Section <a href="#S7" title="7. Reduction to dilation two ‣ Nonnegative Bakry–Émery Curvature on Bounded-Degree Graphs Implies Volume Doubling and Poincaré Inequalities" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a> proves
Theorem <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem3" title="Theorem 1.3. ‣ 1.3. The local Poincaré problem ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Nonnegative Bakry–Émery Curvature on Bounded-Degree Graphs Implies Volume Doubling and Poincaré Inequalities" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.3</span></a>. Appendix <a href="#A1" title="Appendix A Bounded maximum principle on an infinite graph ‣ Nonnegative Bakry–Émery Curvature on Bounded-Degree Graphs Implies Volume Doubling and Poincaré Inequalities" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a> contains the maximum
principle used on infinite graphs.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2. </span>Preliminary</h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Because the maximum degree is bounded, the unnormalised combinatorial Laplacian is already a
bounded operator on <math id="S2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell^{\infty}(V)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell^{\infty}(V)</annotation></semantics></math>. We use <math id="S2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math> as the sole ambient generator in all
curvature and nonlinear-flow arguments, with heat semigroup <math id="S2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{t}=e^{t\Delta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{t}=e^{t\Delta}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Uniformisation is used only to verify the Markov properties of <math id="S2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>. A finite-volume censored
restriction <math id="S2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>D</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{D}</annotation></semantics></math> will be introduced in Section <a href="#S6" title="6. Heat-kernel localisation for Poincaré inequalities ‣ Nonnegative Bakry–Émery Curvature on Bounded-Degree Graphs Implies Volume Doubling and Poincaré Inequalities" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a> solely for
the localisation argument. We also record the <math id="S2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell^{\infty}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell^{\infty}</annotation></semantics></math>-norm continuity and differentiability
needed later, and write <math id="S2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{x}:=\deg(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi>deg</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{x}:=\deg(x)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The cases <math id="S2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{*}\leq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo>≤</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{*}\leq 1</annotation></semantics></math> are immediate: a connected simple graph of maximum degree at most one has at most two vertices. They will be included in the final constant by setting <math id="S2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K(d_{*})=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K(d_{*})=2</annotation></semantics></math> in those cases. Henceforth assume</p>
<table id="S2.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(1)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{*}\geq 2,\qquad N:=d_{*}(d_{*}+3)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{*}\geq 2,\qquad N:=d_{*}(d_{*}+3).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">The symbol <math id="S2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> is an effective parameter for the estimates below; it is not a claim that the graph satisfies the global condition <math id="S2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{CD}(0,N)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>CD</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{CD}(0,N)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Because <math id="S2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{*}&lt;\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{*}&lt;\infty</annotation></semantics></math>, the operator <math id="S2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math> is bounded on <math id="S2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell^{\infty}(V)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell^{\infty}(V)</annotation></semantics></math> and</p>
<table id="S2.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(2)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left\lVert\Delta\right\rVert_{\ell^{\infty}\to\ell^{\infty}}\leq 2d_{*}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msup></mrow></msub><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left\lVert\Delta\right\rVert_{\ell^{\infty}\to\ell^{\infty}}\leq 2d_{*}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p3.2" class="ltx_p">Define the heat semigroup directly by</p>
<table id="S2.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(3)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{t}:=\mathrm{e}^{t\Delta},\qquad t\geq 0." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{t}:=\mathrm{e}^{t\Delta},\qquad t\geq 0.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p3.3" class="ltx_p">Its Markov properties follow, for example, from the uniformisation formula</p>
<table id="S2.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(4)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{t}=\mathrm{e}^{-d_{*}t}\sum_{n=0}^{\infty}\frac{(d_{*}t)^{n}}{n!}\left(I+\frac{\Delta}{d_{*}}\right)^{n}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></munderover><mrow><mfrac><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>n</mi></msup><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>!</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><msub><mi>d</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub></mfrac></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{t}=\mathrm{e}^{-d_{*}t}\sum_{n=0}^{\infty}\frac{(d_{*}t)^{n}}{n!}\left(I+\frac{\Delta}{d_{*}}\right)^{n}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p3.4" class="ltx_p">Indeed, <math id="S2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I+d_{*}^{-1}\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>d</mi><mo>∗</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I+d_{*}^{-1}\Delta</annotation></semantics></math> is the symmetric stochastic operator with edge transition probabilities <math id="S2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{*}^{-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>d</mi><mo>∗</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{*}^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math> and holding probability <math id="S2.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1-d_{x}/d_{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1-d_{x}/d_{*}</annotation></semantics></math> at <math id="S2.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus <math id="S2.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> has a symmetric stochastic kernel <math id="S2.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{t}(x,y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{t}(x,y)</annotation></semantics></math>, is a positive contraction on every <math id="S2.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell^{q}(V)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>q</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell^{q}(V)</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\leq q\leq\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\leq q\leq\infty</annotation></semantics></math>, is self-adjoint on <math id="S2.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell^{2}(V)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell^{2}(V)</annotation></semantics></math>, and satisfies <math id="S2.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{t}\mathbf{1}=\mathbf{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>𝟏</mn></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>𝟏</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{t}\mathbf{1}=\mathbf{1}</annotation></semantics></math>. The use of (<a href="#S2.E4" title="In 2. Preliminary ‣ Nonnegative Bakry–Émery Curvature on Bounded-Degree Graphs Implies Volume Doubling and Poincaré Inequalities" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>) is only to verify these semigroup properties; all subsequent differential and curvature calculations are performed with the single Laplacian <math id="S2.p3.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Because <math id="S2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is connected and locally finite, <math id="S2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math> is countable. Symmetry and stochasticity therefore imply column stochasticity. In particular, for every nonnegative <math id="S2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h\in\ell^{1}(V)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h\in\ell^{1}(V)</annotation></semantics></math>, Tonelli’s theorem gives</p>
<table id="S2.E5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(5)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{x\in V}P_{t}h(x)=\sum_{y\in V}h(y)\sum_{x\in V}p_{t}(x,y)=\sum_{y\in V}h(y)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{x\in V}P_{t}h(x)=\sum_{y\in V}h(y)\sum_{x\in V}p_{t}(x,y)=\sum_{y\in V}h(y).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p4.2" class="ltx_p">All semigroup differentiations below are bounded-operator differentiations on <math id="S2.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell^{\infty}(V)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell^{\infty}(V)</annotation></semantics></math>.
More precisely, <math id="S2.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\mapsto P_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\mapsto P_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> is real analytic in operator norm, and becomes entire after
complexification, with</p>
<table id="S2.Ex23" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex23.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{\,\mathrm{d}}{\,\mathrm{d}t}P_{t}=\Delta P_{t}=P_{t}\Delta." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{\,\mathrm{d}}{\,\mathrm{d}t}P_{t}=\Delta P_{t}=P_{t}\Delta.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p4.3" class="ltx_p">The bilinear maps <math id="S2.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> are bounded:</p>
<table id="S2.EGx1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.E6"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(6)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\left\lVert\Gamma(f,g)\right\rVert_{\infty}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\left\lVert\Gamma(f,g)\right\rVert_{\infty}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\leq 2d_{*}\left\lVert f\right\rVert_{\infty}\left\lVert g\right\rVert_{\infty}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\leq 2d_{*}\left\lVert f\right\rVert_{\infty}\left\lVert g\right\rVert_{\infty},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S2.E7"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(7)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\left\lVert\Gamma_{2}(f,g)\right\rVert_{\infty}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\left\lVert\Gamma_{2}(f,g)\right\rVert_{\infty}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\leq 6d_{*}^{2}\left\lVert f\right\rVert_{\infty}\left\lVert g\right\rVert_{\infty}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mn>6</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>d</mi><mo>∗</mo><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\leq 6d_{*}^{2}\left\lVert f\right\rVert_{\infty}\left\lVert g\right\rVert_{\infty}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p4.4" class="ltx_p">Consequently their associated quadratic maps are <math id="S2.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{\infty}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>C</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{\infty}</annotation></semantics></math>, with</p>
<table id="S2.Ex24" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex24.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D[\Gamma(\,\cdot\,)](u)h=2\Gamma(u,h),\qquad D[\Gamma_{2}(\,\cdot\,)](u)h=2\Gamma_{2}(u,h)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo>⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo>⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D[\Gamma(\,\cdot\,)](u)h=2\Gamma(u,h),\qquad D[\Gamma_{2}(\,\cdot\,)](u)h=2\Gamma_{2}(u,h).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p4.5" class="ltx_p">For every <math id="S2.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma\in\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma\in\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math>, the pointwise exponential map
<math id="S2.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{\gamma}(u):=\mathrm{e}^{\gamma u}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>γ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{\gamma}(u):=\mathrm{e}^{\gamma u}</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S2.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{\infty}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>C</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{\infty}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S2.p4.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell^{\infty}(V)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell^{\infty}(V)</annotation></semantics></math> and</p>
<table id="S2.Ex25" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex25.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="DE_{\gamma}(u)h=\gamma\mathrm{e}^{\gamma u}h." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>γ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">DE_{\gamma}(u)h=\gamma\mathrm{e}^{\gamma u}h.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p4.6" class="ltx_p">Thus all differentiations below are ordinary Fréchet differentiations of norm-<math id="S2.p4.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{1}</annotation></semantics></math>
Banach-valued curves. Every Banach-valued time integral is a Bochner integral in the space
indicated. In particular, the interpolation integrals are <math id="S2.p4.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell^{\infty}(V)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell^{\infty}(V)</annotation></semantics></math>-valued, whereas the
resolvent integral is taken in operator norm in <math id="S2.p4.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{B}(\ell^{\infty}(V))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{B}(\ell^{\infty}(V))</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">The pointwise edge formula for the carré du champ is</p>
<table id="S2.E8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(8)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma(f,g)(x)=\frac{1}{2}\sum_{y\sim x}\bigl(f(y)-f(x)\bigr)\bigl(g(y)-g(x)\bigr)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma(f,g)(x)=\frac{1}{2}\sum_{y\sim x}\bigl(f(y)-f(x)\bigr)\bigl(g(y)-g(x)\bigr).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p5.2" class="ltx_p">We assume <math id="S2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{CD}(0,\infty)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>CD</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{CD}(0,\infty)</annotation></semantics></math> from now on:</p>
<table id="S2.E9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(9)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E9.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\Gamma_{2}(f)(x)\geq 0\qquad(f:V\to\mathbb{R},\ x\in V)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma_{2}(f)(x)\geq 0\qquad(f:V\to\mathbb{R},\ x\in V).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p5.3" class="ltx_p">The first identity below is the defining relation for <math id="S2.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, rewritten:</p>
<table id="S2.E10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(10)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta\Gamma(f)=2\Gamma(f,\Delta f)+2\Gamma_{2}(f)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta\Gamma(f)=2\Gamma(f,\Delta f)+2\Gamma_{2}(f).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p5.4" class="ltx_p">The second is the standard semigroup interpolation identity. See <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite> for the general
<math id="S2.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math>-calculus framework and <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">25</a>]</cite> for graph-specific semigroup formulations. For
bounded <math id="S2.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>,</p>
<table id="S2.E11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(11)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{t}\Gamma(f)-\Gamma(P_{t}f)=2\int_{0}^{t}P_{s}\Gamma_{2}(P_{t-s}f)\,\,\mathrm{d}s." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∫</mo><mn>0</mn><mi>t</mi></msubsup><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.340em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{t}\Gamma(f)-\Gamma(P_{t}f)=2\int_{0}^{t}P_{s}\Gamma_{2}(P_{t-s}f)\,\,\mathrm{d}s.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p5.5" class="ltx_p">Indeed, differentiating in <math id="S2.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell^{\infty}(V)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell^{\infty}(V)</annotation></semantics></math> the function</p>
<table id="S2.Ex26" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex26.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi(s):=P_{s}\Gamma(P_{t-s}f)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi(s):=P_{s}\Gamma(P_{t-s}f)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p5.6" class="ltx_p">gives <math id="S2.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi^{\prime}(s)=2P_{s}\Gamma_{2}(P_{t-s}f)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi^{\prime}(s)=2P_{s}\Gamma_{2}(P_{t-s}f)</annotation></semantics></math>. The integrand in (<a href="#S2.E11" title="In 2. Preliminary ‣ Nonnegative Bakry–Émery Curvature on Bounded-Degree Graphs Implies Volume Doubling and Poincaré Inequalities" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a>) is norm-continuous: <math id="S2.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s\mapsto P_{t-s}f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s\mapsto P_{t-s}f</annotation></semantics></math> is norm-continuous and <math id="S2.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> is a continuous quadratic map from <math id="S2.p5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell^{\infty}(V)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell^{\infty}(V)</annotation></semantics></math> to itself because <math id="S2.p5.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math> is bounded. Thus the integral is an ordinary Bochner integral in <math id="S2.p5.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell^{\infty}(V)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell^{\infty}(V)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3. </span>Point-mass estimates and resolvent smoothing</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This section extracts the two finite-dimensional substitutes used in the volume and localisation
arguments. First, a point-mass test of the positive semidefinite form
<math id="S3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma_{2}(\cdot,\cdot)(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma_{2}(\cdot,\cdot)(x)</annotation></semantics></math> gives a second-order coercive inequality and an effective dimension
estimate at a negative local minimum of <math id="S3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta f</annotation></semantics></math>. Second, a positive resolvent turns the same
coercive expression into a global <math id="S3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L^{\infty}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>L</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L^{\infty}</annotation></semantics></math> estimate for <math id="S3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta P_{t}f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta P_{t}f</annotation></semantics></math>. The first estimate is
used in the Li–Yau maximum principle, and the second controls semigroup displacement and
exit-time localisation.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1. </span>Point-mass estimates</h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in V</annotation></semantics></math>, write <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{x}:=\mathbf{1}_{\{x\}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><msub><mn>𝟏</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{x}:=\mathbf{1}_{\{x\}}</annotation></semantics></math>. All quantities in the next lemma depend only on the restriction of <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> to the two-ball about <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>, so no boundedness assumption on <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> is needed. The identity is the algebraic core of the proof.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 3.1</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Point-mass identities)<span id="S3.Thmtheorem1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For every <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in V</annotation></semantics></math> and every function <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:V\to\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:V\to\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math>,</span></p>
