</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p4.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p4.2.1" class="ltx_text">is nonnegative and satisfies</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex41" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex41.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}h(x)=\sum_{y\in V}r(x,y)h(y)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}h(x)=\sum_{y\in V}r(x,y)h(y)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p4.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p4.3.1" class="ltx_text">for bounded <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math>; Tonelli’s theorem, applied first to <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left\lvert h\right\rvert" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>|</mo><mi>h</mi><mo>|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left\lvert h\right\rvert</annotation></semantics></math>, justifies the interchange. Finally, <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{s}\mathbf{1}=\mathbf{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>𝟏</mn></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>𝟏</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{s}\mathbf{1}=\mathbf{1}</annotation></semantics></math> gives (<a href="#S3.E22" title="In Lemma 3.4 (Positive resolvent). ‣ 3.2. Resolvent smoothing ‣ 3. Point-mass estimates and resolvent smoothing ‣ Nonnegative Bakry–Émery Curvature on Bounded-Degree Graphs Implies Volume Doubling and Poincaré Inequalities" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">22</span></a>), equivalently</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex42" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

