<div id="S3.Thmtheorem5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 3.5</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Kernel Cauchy–Schwarz)<span id="S3.Thmtheorem5.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S3.Thmtheorem5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝖪</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{K}</annotation></semantics></math> be a positive kernel operator on <math id="S3.Thmtheorem5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math> with finite row masses. Then, for every
bounded real-valued <math id="S3.Thmtheorem5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math>,</span></p>
<table id="S3.E24" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(24)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E24.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathsf{K}h)^{2}\leq(\mathsf{K}\mathbf{1})\mathsf{K}(h^{2})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝖪</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝖪</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>𝟏</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝖪</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>h</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathsf{K}h)^{2}\leq(\mathsf{K}\mathbf{1})\mathsf{K}(h^{2})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem5.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem5.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">pointwise on <math id="S3.Thmtheorem5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.3" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S3.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text">Write <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{K}h(x)=\sum_{y}k(x,y)h(y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝖪</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>y</mi></msub><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{K}h(x)=\sum_{y}k(x,y)h(y)</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k(x,y)\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k(x,y)\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math>. At fixed <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>,
Cauchy–Schwarz in the finite measure <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{y}k(x,y)\delta_{y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>y</mi></msub><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>y</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{y}k(x,y)\delta_{y}</annotation></semantics></math> gives</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex43" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex43.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left\lvert\mathsf{K}h(x)\right\rvert^{2}\leq\left(\sum_{y}k(x,y)\right)\left(\sum_{y}k(x,y)h(y)^{2}\right)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mrow><mo>|</mo><mrow><mi>𝖪</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>|</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><munder><mo lspace="0em" movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mi>y</mi></munder><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><munder><mo lspace="0em" movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mi>y</mi></munder><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left\lvert\mathsf{K}h(x)\right\rvert^{2}\leq\left(\sum_{y}k(x,y)\right)\left(\sum_{y}k(x,y)h(y)^{2}\right),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p5.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p5.2.1" class="ltx_text">which is (<a href="#S3.E24" title="In Lemma 3.5 (Kernel Cauchy–Schwarz). ‣ 3.2. Resolvent smoothing ‣ 3. Point-mass estimates and resolvent smoothing ‣ Nonnegative Bakry–Émery Curvature on Bounded-Degree Graphs Implies Volume Doubling and Poincaré Inequalities" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">24</span></a>).
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem6" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 3.6</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem6.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Laplacian smoothing)<span id="S3.Thmtheorem6.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For every bounded <math id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:V\to\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:V\to\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math>, every <math id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, and every <math id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in V</annotation></semantics></math>,</span></p>
<table id="S3.E25" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(25)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E25.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left\lvert\Delta P_{t}f(x)\right\rvert^{2}\leq\frac{N}{t}\mathcal{R}\bigl(P_{t}\Gamma(f)-\Gamma(P_{t}f)\bigr)(x)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mrow><mo>|</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>|</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mfrac><mi>N</mi><mi>t</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left\lvert\Delta P_{t}f(x)\right\rvert^{2}\leq\frac{N}{t}\mathcal{R}\bigl(P_{t}\Gamma(f)-\Gamma(P_{t}f)\bigr)(x).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">In particular,</span></p>
<table id="S3.E26" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(26)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E26.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left\lVert\Delta P_{t}f\right\rVert_{\infty}^{2}\leq\frac{N}{2t}\left\lVert\Gamma(f)\right\rVert_{\infty}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mfrac><mi>N</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left\lVert\Delta P_{t}f\right\rVert_{\infty}^{2}\leq\frac{N}{2t}\left\lVert\Gamma(f)\right\rVert_{\infty}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p1.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Consequently,</span></p>
<table id="S3.E27" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(27)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E27.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left\lVert P_{t}f-f\right\rVert_{\infty}\leq\sqrt{2Nt\,\left\lVert\Gamma(f)\right\rVert_{\infty}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub><mo>≤</mo><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub></mrow></msqrt></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left\lVert P_{t}f-f\right\rVert_{\infty}\leq\sqrt{2Nt\,\left\lVert\Gamma(f)\right\rVert_{\infty}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.4" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S3.SS2.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text">Fix <math id="S3.SS2.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s\in[0,t]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s\in[0,t]</annotation></semantics></math> and set <math id="S3.SS2.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{s}:=P_{t-s}f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{s}:=P_{t-s}f</annotation></semantics></math>. Since <math id="S3.SS2.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{s}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}</annotation></semantics></math> are bounded
functions of <math id="S3.SS2.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math>, all three relevant operators commute, and</span></p>
<table id="S3.E28" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(28)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E28.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="AP_{t}f=P_{s}Ah_{s}=\mathcal{R}P_{s}\mathcal{T}h_{s}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">AP_{t}f=P_{s}Ah_{s}=\mathcal{R}P_{s}\mathcal{T}h_{s}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p6.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p6.2.1" class="ltx_text">Here <math id="S3.SS2.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{s}Ah_{s}=AP_{t}f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{s}Ah_{s}=AP_{t}f</annotation></semantics></math> follows from the semigroup property, while
<math id="S3.SS2.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}\mathcal{T}=\mathcal{R}A(A+2I)=A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}\mathcal{T}=\mathcal{R}A(A+2I)=A</annotation></semantics></math> because the factors commute.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text">For clarity, the positive kernel of <math id="S3.SS2.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}P_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}P_{s}</annotation></semantics></math> is</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex44" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex44.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{s}(x,y)=\int_{0}^{\infty}\mathrm{e}^{-2u}p_{u+s}(x,y)\,\,\mathrm{d}u," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∫</mo><mn>0</mn><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msubsup><mrow><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.340em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{s}(x,y)=\int_{0}^{\infty}\mathrm{e}^{-2u}p_{u+s}(x,y)\,\,\mathrm{d}u,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p7.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p7.2.1" class="ltx_text">and its row mass is exactly <math id="S3.SS2.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1/2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1/2</annotation></semantics></math>. Apply Lemma <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem5" title="Lemma 3.5 (Kernel Cauchy–Schwarz). ‣ 3.2. Resolvent smoothing ‣ 3. Point-mass estimates and resolvent smoothing ‣ Nonnegative Bakry–Émery Curvature on Bounded-Degree Graphs Implies Volume Doubling and Poincaré Inequalities" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.5</span></a> to
<math id="S3.SS2.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{K}=\mathcal{R}P_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝖪</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{K}=\mathcal{R}P_{s}</annotation></semantics></math>. By Proposition <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem2" title="Proposition 3.2 (Second-order coercivity). ‣ 3.1. Point-mass estimates ‣ 3. Point-mass estimates and resolvent smoothing ‣ Nonnegative Bakry–Émery Curvature on Bounded-Degree Graphs Implies Volume Doubling and Poincaré Inequalities" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a>,
<math id="S3.SS2.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathcal{T}h_{s})^{2}\leq 4N\Gamma_{2}(h_{s})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathcal{T}h_{s})^{2}\leq 4N\Gamma_{2}(h_{s})</annotation></semantics></math>. Hence, pointwise,</span></p>
<table id="S3.E29" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(29)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E29.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(AP_{t}f)^{2}\leq\frac{1}{2}\mathcal{R}P_{s}\bigl((\mathcal{T}h_{s})^{2}\bigr)\leq 2N\,\mathcal{R}P_{s}\Gamma_{2}(P_{t-s}f)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(AP_{t}f)^{2}\leq\frac{1}{2}\mathcal{R}P_{s}\bigl((\mathcal{T}h_{s})^{2}\bigr)\leq 2N\,\mathcal{R}P_{s}\Gamma_{2}(P_{t-s}f).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p7.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p7.3.1" class="ltx_text">The left-hand side does not depend on <math id="S3.SS2.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math>. The map
<math id="S3.SS2.p7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s\mapsto P_{s}\Gamma_{2}(P_{t-s}f)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s\mapsto P_{s}\Gamma_{2}(P_{t-s}f)</annotation></semantics></math> is norm-continuous in <math id="S3.SS2.p7.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell^{\infty}(V)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell^{\infty}(V)</annotation></semantics></math>, and
<math id="S3.SS2.p7.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}</annotation></semantics></math> is bounded, so integrating (<a href="#S3.E29" title="In Proof. ‣ 3.2. Resolvent smoothing ‣ 3. Point-mass estimates and resolvent smoothing ‣ Nonnegative Bakry–Émery Curvature on Bounded-Degree Graphs Implies Volume Doubling and Poincaré Inequalities" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">29</span></a>) as a Bochner
integral and using (<a href="#S2.E11" title="In 2. Preliminary ‣ Nonnegative Bakry–Émery Curvature on Bounded-Degree Graphs Implies Volume Doubling and Poincaré Inequalities" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a>) gives</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex45" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex45.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t(AP_{t}f)^{2}\leq 2N\mathcal{R}\int_{0}^{t}P_{s}\Gamma_{2}(P_{t-s}f)\,\,\mathrm{d}s=N\mathcal{R}\bigl(P_{t}\Gamma(f)-\Gamma(P_{t}f)\bigr)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∫</mo><mn>0</mn><mi>t</mi></msubsup><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.340em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t(AP_{t}f)^{2}\leq 2N\mathcal{R}\int_{0}^{t}P_{s}\Gamma_{2}(P_{t-s}f)\,\,\mathrm{d}s=N\mathcal{R}\bigl(P_{t}\Gamma(f)-\Gamma(P_{t}f)\bigr).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p7.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p7.4.1" class="ltx_text">This is (<a href="#S3.E25" title="In Theorem 3.6 (Laplacian smoothing). ‣ 3.2. Resolvent smoothing ‣ 3. Point-mass estimates and resolvent smoothing ‣ Nonnegative Bakry–Émery Curvature on Bounded-Degree Graphs Implies Volume Doubling and Poincaré Inequalities" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">25</span></a>), because <math id="S3.SS2.p7.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A=-\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A=-\Delta</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p8.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p8.1.1" class="ltx_text">Under <math id="S3.SS2.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{CD}(0,\infty)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>CD</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{CD}(0,\infty)</annotation></semantics></math> the bracket in (<a href="#S3.E25" title="In Theorem 3.6 (Laplacian smoothing). ‣ 3.2. Resolvent smoothing ‣ 3. Point-mass estimates and resolvent smoothing ‣ Nonnegative Bakry–Émery Curvature on Bounded-Degree Graphs Implies Volume Doubling and Poincaré Inequalities" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">25</span></a>) is
nonnegative by (<a href="#S2.E11" title="In 2. Preliminary ‣ Nonnegative Bakry–Émery Curvature on Bounded-Degree Graphs Implies Volume Doubling and Poincaré Inequalities" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a>). Since <math id="S3.SS2.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma(P_{t}f)\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma(P_{t}f)\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}</annotation></semantics></math> is
positive,</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex46" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex46.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\leq\mathcal{R}\bigl(P_{t}\Gamma(f)-\Gamma(P_{t}f)\bigr)\leq\mathcal{R}P_{t}\Gamma(f)\leq\frac{1}{2}\left\lVert\Gamma(f)\right\rVert_{\infty}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\leq\mathcal{R}\bigl(P_{t}\Gamma(f)-\Gamma(P_{t}f)\bigr)\leq\mathcal{R}P_{t}\Gamma(f)\leq\frac{1}{2}\left\lVert\Gamma(f)\right\rVert_{\infty},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p8.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p8.2.1" class="ltx_text">where the last inequality uses positivity, <math id="S3.SS2.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{t}\mathbf{1}=\mathbf{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>𝟏</mn></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>𝟏</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{t}\mathbf{1}=\mathbf{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, and
(<a href="#S3.E22" title="In Lemma 3.4 (Positive resolvent). ‣ 3.2. Resolvent smoothing ‣ 3. Point-mass estimates and resolvent smoothing ‣ Nonnegative Bakry–Émery Curvature on Bounded-Degree Graphs Implies Volume Doubling and Poincaré Inequalities" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">22</span></a>). This proves (<a href="#S3.E26" title="In Theorem 3.6 (Laplacian smoothing). ‣ 3.2. Resolvent smoothing ‣ 3. Point-mass estimates and resolvent smoothing ‣ Nonnegative Bakry–Émery Curvature on Bounded-Degree Graphs Implies Volume Doubling and Poincaré Inequalities" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">26</span></a>). Finally,</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex47" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex47.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{t}f-f=\int_{0}^{t}\Delta P_{s}f\,\,\mathrm{d}s" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∫</mo><mn>0</mn><mi>t</mi></msubsup><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.340em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{t}f-f=\int_{0}^{t}\Delta P_{s}f\,\,\mathrm{d}s</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p8.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p8.3.1" class="ltx_text">in <math id="S3.SS2.p8.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell^{\infty}(V)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell^{\infty}(V)</annotation></semantics></math>, and therefore</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex48" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex48.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left\lVert P_{t}f-f\right\rVert_{\infty}\leq\int_{0}^{t}\sqrt{\frac{N}{2s}\left\lVert\Gamma(f)\right\rVert_{\infty}}\,\,\mathrm{d}s=\sqrt{2Nt\,\left\lVert\Gamma(f)\right\rVert_{\infty}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">≤</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∫</mo><mn>0</mn><mi>t</mi></msubsup><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mfrac><mi>N</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub></mrow></msqrt><mo lspace="0.340em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub></mrow></msqrt></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left\lVert P_{t}f-f\right\rVert_{\infty}\leq\int_{0}^{t}\sqrt{\frac{N}{2s}\left\lVert\Gamma(f)\right\rVert_{\infty}}\,\,\mathrm{d}s=\sqrt{2Nt\,\left\lVert\Gamma(f)\right\rVert_{\infty}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p8.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p8.4.1" class="ltx_text">∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmremarkx3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmremarkx3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Remark</span></span><span id="Thmremarkx3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmremarkx3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmremarkx3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmremarkx3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Neither (<a href="#S3.E17" title="In Proposition 3.3 (Extremal curvature–dimension inequality). ‣ 3.1. Point-mass estimates ‣ 3. Point-mass estimates and resolvent smoothing ‣ Nonnegative Bakry–Émery Curvature on Bounded-Degree Graphs Implies Volume Doubling and Poincaré Inequalities" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">17</span></a>) nor (<a href="#S3.E26" title="In Theorem 3.6 (Laplacian smoothing). ‣ 3.2. Resolvent smoothing ‣ 3. Point-mass estimates and resolvent smoothing ‣ Nonnegative Bakry–Émery Curvature on Bounded-Degree Graphs Implies Volume Doubling and Poincaré Inequalities" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">26</span></a>) implies a global <math id="Thmremarkx3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{CD}(0,N)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>CD</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{CD}(0,N)</annotation></semantics></math> inequality. They are instead two projections of the same positive-semidefinite structure onto the point mass <math id="Thmremarkx3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{x}</annotation></semantics></math>. The first is adapted to a maximum principle; the second is adapted to semigroup interpolation through the factorisation <math id="Thmremarkx3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}=A(A+2I)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}=A(A+2I)</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4. </span>Modified heat, Li–Yau, and Harnack estimates</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This section develops the analytic route from the point-mass estimates to Harnack control.
The modified heat equation replaces the unavailable diffusion chain rule; its gradient bound
and exponential comparisons are then combined with the extremal curvature estimate from
Subsection <a href="#S3.SS1" title="3.1. Point-mass estimates ‣ 3. Point-mass estimates and resolvent smoothing ‣ Nonnegative Bakry–Émery Curvature on Bounded-Degree Graphs Implies Volume Doubling and Poincaré Inequalities" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>. The output is a Li–Yau inequality and a modified-flow Harnack
estimate, which are the final analytic inputs for volume doubling.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1. </span>The modified heat equation</h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Münch introduced, for finite graphs, the use of</p>
<table id="S4.Ex49" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex49.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial_{t}u=\Delta u+\Gamma(u)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∂</mo><mi>t</mi></msub><mi>u</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial_{t}u=\Delta u+\Gamma(u)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">as a discrete substitute for <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\log(P_{t}\mathrm{e}^{u_{0}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\log(P_{t}\mathrm{e}^{u_{0}})</annotation></semantics></math>, together with the gradient estimate and
pointwise comparisons with the linear heat semigroup
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">31</a>, Theorems 2.1 and 2.3]</cite>. Pajot and Russ developed the corresponding
<math id="S4.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L^{\infty}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>L</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L^{\infty}</annotation></semantics></math> theory on possibly infinite weighted graphs of bounded geometry. Using linear
semigroup estimates and a convergent mild iteration, they proved global well-posedness, treated
the failure of spatial extrema to be attained, and extended Münch’s comparison argument
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">34</a>, Theorem 1.3 and Sections 5–6]</cite>. In the present bounded-degree unnormalised
setting, the vector field <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u\mapsto\Delta u+\Gamma(u)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u\mapsto\Delta u+\Gamma(u)</annotation></semantics></math> is locally Lipschitz on
<math id="S4.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell^{\infty}(V)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell^{\infty}(V)</annotation></semantics></math>, so we replace their mild-iteration construction by a direct Banach-space ODE
argument.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">For bounded initial data <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>, consider</p>
<table id="S4.E30" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(30)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E30.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial_{t}u_{t}=\Delta u_{t}+\Gamma(u_{t}),\qquad u_{t=0}=u_{0}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∂</mo><mi>t</mi></msub><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial_{t}u_{t}=\Delta u_{t}+\Gamma(u_{t}),\qquad u_{t=0}=u_{0}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">The next proposition records global existence and the gradient maximum principle in the present bounded-degree setting.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 4.1</span></span><span id="S4.Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Global nonlinear flow and gradient bound)<span id="S4.Thmtheorem1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{0}\in\ell^{\infty}(V)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{0}\in\ell^{\infty}(V)</annotation></semantics></math> and assume</span></p>
<table id="S4.E31" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(31)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E31.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left\lVert\Gamma(u_{0})\right\rVert_{\infty}&lt;\frac{1}{2}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left\lVert\Gamma(u_{0})\right\rVert_{\infty}&lt;\frac{1}{2}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Then (<a href="#S4.E30" title="In 4.1. The modified heat equation ‣ 4. Modified heat, Li–Yau, and Harnack estimates ‣ Nonnegative Bakry–Émery Curvature on Bounded-Degree Graphs Implies Volume Doubling and Poincaré Inequalities" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">30</span></a>) has a unique global solution</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex50" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex50.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u\in C^{1}\bigl([0,\infty),\ell^{\infty}(V)\bigr)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u\in C^{1}\bigl([0,\infty),\ell^{\infty}(V)\bigr).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Moreover,</span></p>
<table id="S4.E32" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(32)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E32.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left\lVert\Gamma(u_{t})\right\rVert_{\infty}\leq\left\lVert\Gamma(u_{0})\right\rVert_{\infty}\qquad(t\geq 0)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub><mo>≤</mo><msub><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub></mrow><mspace style="width:2em;" width="2em"></mspace><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left\lVert\Gamma(u_{t})\right\rVert_{\infty}\leq\left\lVert\Gamma(u_{0})\right\rVert_{\infty}\qquad(t\geq 0),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">and hence</span></p>
<table id="S4.E33" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(33)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E33.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left\lvert u_{t}(y)-u_{t}(x)\right\rvert&lt;1\qquad(x\sim y,\ t\geq 0)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo>|</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>|</mo></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mspace style="width:2em;" width="2em"></mspace><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left\lvert u_{t}(y)-u_{t}(x)\right\rvert&lt;1\qquad(x\sim y,\ t\geq 0).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S4.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text">Set</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex51" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex51.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}(u):=\Delta u+\Gamma(u)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}(u):=\Delta u+\Gamma(u).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.p3.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p3.2.1" class="ltx_text">This is a <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{\infty}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>C</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{\infty}</annotation></semantics></math> quadratic polynomial on <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell^{\infty}(V)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell^{\infty}(V)</annotation></semantics></math>, with</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex52" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex52.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D\mathcal{F}(u)h=\Delta h+2\Gamma(u,h)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D\mathcal{F}(u)h=\Delta h+2\Gamma(u,h).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.p3.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p3.3.1" class="ltx_text">In particular, <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}</annotation></semantics></math> is locally Lipschitz. Indeed, if <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left\lVert u\right\rVert_{\infty},\left\lVert v\right\rVert_{\infty}\leq M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub><mo>≤</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left\lVert u\right\rVert_{\infty},\left\lVert v\right\rVert_{\infty}\leq M</annotation></semantics></math>, then for every <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>,</span></p>
<table id="S4.EGx4" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.Ex53"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex53.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\left\lvert\Gamma(u)(x)-\Gamma(v)(x)\right\rvert" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>|</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\left\lvert\Gamma(u)(x)-\Gamma(v)(x)\right\rvert</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex53.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\leq\frac{1}{2}\sum_{y\sim x}\left\lvert(u(y)-u(x))^{2}-(v(y)-v(x))^{2}\right\rvert" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></munder></mstyle><mrow><mo>|</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>|</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\leq\frac{1}{2}\sum_{y\sim x}\left\lvert(u(y)-u(x))^{2}-(v(y)-v(x))^{2}\right\rvert</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.Ex54"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex54.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\leq 4d_{*}M\left\lVert u-v\right\rVert_{\infty}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\leq 4d_{*}M\left\lVert u-v\right\rVert_{\infty}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.p3.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p3.4.1" class="ltx_text">Together with (<a href="#S2.E2" title="In 2. Preliminary ‣ Nonnegative Bakry–Émery Curvature on Bounded-Degree Graphs Implies Volume Doubling and Poincaré Inequalities" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>), this gives</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex55" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex55.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left\lVert\mathcal{F}(u)-\mathcal{F}(v)\right\rVert_{\infty}\leq\bigl(2d_{*}+4d_{*}M\bigr)\left\lVert u-v\right\rVert_{\infty}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left\lVert\mathcal{F}(u)-\mathcal{F}(v)\right\rVert_{\infty}\leq\bigl(2d_{*}+4d_{*}M\bigr)\left\lVert u-v\right\rVert_{\infty}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.p3.5" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p3.5.1" class="ltx_text">on the closed radius-<math id="S4.SS1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> ball. The Banach-space Picard–Lindelöf theorem
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite> therefore gives a unique maximal solution</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex56" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex56.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u\in C^{1}\bigl([0,T_{\max}),\ell^{\infty}(V)\bigr)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u\in C^{1}\bigl([0,T_{\max}),\ell^{\infty}(V)\bigr).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.p3.6" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p3.6.1" class="ltx_text">We postpone the continuation argument until after deriving the a priori bounds below.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text">Let</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex57" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex57.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{t}:=\Gamma(u_{t})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{t}:=\Gamma(u_{t}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.p4.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p4.2.1" class="ltx_text">The bilinearity and continuity of <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math> show that <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H\in C^{1}([0,T_{\max}),\ell^{\infty}(V))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H\in C^{1}([0,T_{\max}),\ell^{\infty}(V))</annotation></semantics></math>. Differentiating and using (<a href="#S2.E10" title="In 2. Preliminary ‣ Nonnegative Bakry–Émery Curvature on Bounded-Degree Graphs Implies Volume Doubling and Poincaré Inequalities" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a>) gives</span></p>
<table id="S4.EGx5" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.Ex58"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex58.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\partial_{t}H_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∂</mo><mi>t</mi></msub><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\partial_{t}H_{t}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex58.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=2\Gamma(u_{t},\Delta u_{t})+2\Gamma(u_{t},H_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=2\Gamma(u_{t},\Delta u_{t})+2\Gamma(u_{t},H_{t})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.E34"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(34)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E34.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\Delta H_{t}+2\Gamma(u_{t},H_{t})-2\Gamma_{2}(u_{t})." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\Delta H_{t}+2\Gamma(u_{t},H_{t})-2\Gamma_{2}(u_{t}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.p4.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p4.3.1" class="ltx_text">Suppose for the moment that <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left\lVert H_{t}\right\rVert_{\infty}&lt;1/2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo>‖</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left\lVert H_{t}\right\rVert_{\infty}&lt;1/2</annotation></semantics></math>. For every edge <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\sim y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\sim y</annotation></semantics></math>, the single-edge contribution to (<a href="#S2.E8" title="In 2. Preliminary ‣ Nonnegative Bakry–Émery Curvature on Bounded-Degree Graphs Implies Volume Doubling and Poincaré Inequalities" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>) gives</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex59" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex59.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{1}{2}\left\lvert u_{t}(y)-u_{t}(x)\right\rvert^{2}\leq H_{t}(x)&lt;\frac{1}{2}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo>|</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>|</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{1}{2}\left\lvert u_{t}(y)-u_{t}(x)\right\rvert^{2}\leq H_{t}(x)&lt;\frac{1}{2},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.p4.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p4.4.1" class="ltx_text">so <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left\lvert u_{t}(y)-u_{t}(x)\right\rvert&lt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>|</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>|</mo></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left\lvert u_{t}(y)-u_{t}(x)\right\rvert&lt;1</annotation></semantics></math>. Since <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma_{2}(u_{t})\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma_{2}(u_{t})\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math>, equation (<a href="#S4.E34" title="In Proof. ‣ 4.1. The modified heat equation ‣ 4. Modified heat, Li–Yau, and Harnack estimates ‣ Nonnegative Bakry–Émery Curvature on Bounded-Degree Graphs Implies Volume Doubling and Poincaré Inequalities" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">34</span></a>) becomes</span></p>
<table id="S4.E35" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(35)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E35.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial_{t}H_{t}(x)\leq\sum_{y\sim x}\bigl(1+u_{t}(y)-u_{t}(x)\bigr)\bigl(H_{t}(y)-H_{t}(x)\bigr)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∂</mo><mi>t</mi></msub><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">≤</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial_{t}H_{t}(x)\leq\sum_{y\sim x}\bigl(1+u_{t}(y)-u_{t}(x)\bigr)\bigl(H_{t}(y)-H_{t}(x)\bigr).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.p4.5" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p4.5.1" class="ltx_text">For <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y\sim x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y\sim x</annotation></semantics></math>, put</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex60" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex60.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{t}(x,y):=1+u_{t}(y)-u_{t}(x)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{t}(x,y):=1+u_{t}(y)-u_{t}(x),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.p4.6" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p4.6.1" class="ltx_text">and set <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{t}(x,y):=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{t}(x,y):=0</annotation></semantics></math> otherwise. These coefficients are nonnegative, and</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex61" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex61.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{y}a_{t}(x,y)\leq 2d_{x}\leq 2d_{*}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mi>y</mi></munder><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>x</mi></msub></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{y}a_{t}(x,y)\leq 2d_{x}\leq 2d_{*}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.p4.7" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p4.7.1" class="ltx_text">All sums are absolutely convergent because each row has finite support. Lemma <a href="#A1.Thmtheorem1" title="Lemma A.1 (Bounded maximum principle). ‣ Appendix A Bounded maximum principle on an infinite graph ‣ Nonnegative Bakry–Émery Curvature on Bounded-Degree Graphs Implies Volume Doubling and Poincaré Inequalities" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.1</span></a> implies that <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\mapsto\sup_{x}H_{t}(x)=\left\lVert H_{t}\right\rVert_{\infty}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo rspace="0.1389em" stretchy="false">↦</mo><mrow><msub><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.167em">sup</mo><mi>x</mi></msub><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mrow><mo>‖</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\mapsto\sup_{x}H_{t}(x)=\left\lVert H_{t}\right\rVert_{\infty}</annotation></semantics></math> is nonincreasing throughout every time interval on which <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left\lVert H_{t}\right\rVert_{\infty}&lt;1/2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo>‖</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left\lVert H_{t}\right\rVert_{\infty}&lt;1/2</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text">We now close the bootstrap. Let <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{\Gamma}:=\left\lVert H_{0}\right\rVert_{\infty}&lt;1/2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><msub><mrow><mo>‖</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{\Gamma}:=\left\lVert H_{0}\right\rVert_{\infty}&lt;1/2</annotation></semantics></math> and suppose that a first time <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau&lt;T_{\max}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>max</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau&lt;T_{\max}</annotation></semantics></math> exists with <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left\lVert H_{\tau}\right\rVert_{\infty}=1/2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo>‖</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left\lVert H_{\tau}\right\rVert_{\infty}=1/2</annotation></semantics></math>. On <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[0,\tau)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[0,\tau)</annotation></semantics></math> the preceding maximum principle gives <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left\lVert H_{t}\right\rVert_{\infty}\leq M_{\Gamma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo>‖</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left\lVert H_{t}\right\rVert_{\infty}\leq M_{\Gamma}</annotation></semantics></math>. Norm-continuity of <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> then implies <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left\lVert H_{\tau}\right\rVert_{\infty}\leq M_{\Gamma}&lt;1/2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo>‖</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left\lVert H_{\tau}\right\rVert_{\infty}\leq M_{\Gamma}&lt;1/2</annotation></semantics></math>, a contradiction. Therefore (<a href="#S4.E32" title="In Proposition 4.1 (Global nonlinear flow and gradient bound). ‣ 4.1. The modified heat equation ‣ 4. Modified heat, Li–Yau, and Harnack estimates ‣ Nonnegative Bakry–Émery Curvature on Bounded-Degree Graphs Implies Volume Doubling and Poincaré Inequalities" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">32</span></a>) holds on <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[0,T_{\max})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[0,T_{\max})</annotation></semantics></math>, and (<a href="#S4.E33" title="In Proposition 4.1 (Global nonlinear flow and gradient bound). ‣ 4.1. The modified heat equation ‣ 4. Modified heat, Li–Yau, and Harnack estimates ‣ Nonnegative Bakry–Émery Curvature on Bounded-Degree Graphs Implies Volume Doubling and Poincaré Inequalities" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">33</span></a>) follows from the single-edge bound above.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text">Finally,</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex62" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex62.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left\lVert\partial_{t}u_{t}\right\rVert_{\infty}\leq 2d_{*}\left\lVert u_{t}\right\rVert_{\infty}+M_{\Gamma}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><msub><mo lspace="0em">∂</mo><mi>t</mi></msub><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo>‖</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left\lVert\partial_{t}u_{t}\right\rVert_{\infty}\leq 2d_{*}\left\lVert u_{t}\right\rVert_{\infty}+M_{\Gamma}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.p6.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p6.2.1" class="ltx_text">Hence</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex63" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex63.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left\lVert u_{t}\right\rVert_{\infty}\leq\left\lVert u_{0}\right\rVert_{\infty}+\int_{0}^{t}\bigl(2d_{*}\left\lVert u_{s}\right\rVert_{\infty}+M_{\Gamma}\bigr)\,\,\mathrm{d}s." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mo>‖</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msub><mrow><mo>‖</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.055em">+</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo rspace="0em">∫</mo><mn>0</mn><mi>t</mi></msubsup><mrow><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo>‖</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></msub></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.340em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left\lVert u_{t}\right\rVert_{\infty}\leq\left\lVert u_{0}\right\rVert_{\infty}+\int_{0}^{t}\bigl(2d_{*}\left\lVert u_{s}\right\rVert_{\infty}+M_{\Gamma}\bigr)\,\,\mathrm{d}s.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.p6.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p6.3.1" class="ltx_text">Gronwall’s inequality gives</span></p>
<table id="S4.E36" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(36)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E36.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left\lVert u_{t}\right\rVert_{\infty}\leq\mathrm{e}^{2d_{*}t}\left\lVert u_{0}\right\rVert_{\infty}+\frac{M_{\Gamma}}{2d_{*}}\bigl(\mathrm{e}^{2d_{*}t}-1\bigr)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mo>‖</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo>‖</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mfrac><msub><mi>M</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></msub><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left\lVert u_{t}\right\rVert_{\infty}\leq\mathrm{e}^{2d_{*}t}\left\lVert u_{0}\right\rVert_{\infty}+\frac{M_{\Gamma}}{2d_{*}}\bigl(\mathrm{e}^{2d_{*}t}-1\bigr).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.p6.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p6.4.1" class="ltx_text">Suppose, for contradiction, that <math id="S4.SS1.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{\max}&lt;\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{\max}&lt;\infty</annotation></semantics></math>. By
(<a href="#S4.E36" title="In Proof. ‣ 4.1. The modified heat equation ‣ 4. Modified heat, Li–Yau, and Harnack estimates ‣ Nonnegative Bakry–Émery Curvature on Bounded-Degree Graphs Implies Volume Doubling and Poincaré Inequalities" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">36</span></a>),</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex64" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex64.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M:=\sup_{0\leq t&lt;T_{\max}}\left\lVert u_{t}\right\rVert_{\infty}&lt;\infty." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo rspace="0.1389em">:=</mo><mrow><munder><mo lspace="0.1389em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">sup</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>max</mi></msub></mrow></munder><msub><mrow><mo>‖</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M:=\sup_{0\leq t&lt;T_{\max}}\left\lVert u_{t}\right\rVert_{\infty}&lt;\infty.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.p6.5" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p6.5.1" class="ltx_text">Together with (<a href="#S4.E32" title="In Proposition 4.1 (Global nonlinear flow and gradient bound). ‣ 4.1. The modified heat equation ‣ 4. Modified heat, Li–Yau, and Harnack estimates ‣ Nonnegative Bakry–Émery Curvature on Bounded-Degree Graphs Implies Volume Doubling and Poincaré Inequalities" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">32</span></a>), this gives</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex65" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex65.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left\lVert\partial_{t}u_{t}\right\rVert_{\infty}=\left\lVert\Delta u_{t}+\Gamma(u_{t})\right\rVert_{\infty}\leq 2d_{*}M+M_{\Gamma}\qquad(0\leq t&lt;T_{\max})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><msub><mo lspace="0em">∂</mo><mi>t</mi></msub><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:2em;" width="2em"></mspace><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>max</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left\lVert\partial_{t}u_{t}\right\rVert_{\infty}=\left\lVert\Delta u_{t}+\Gamma(u_{t})\right\rVert_{\infty}\leq 2d_{*}M+M_{\Gamma}\qquad(0\leq t&lt;T_{\max}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.p6.6" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p6.6.1" class="ltx_text">Thus <math id="S4.SS1.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math> is uniformly Lipschitz on <math id="S4.SS1.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[0,T_{\max})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[0,T_{\max})</annotation></semantics></math> and consequently has a limit
<math id="S4.SS1.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{*}\in\ell^{\infty}(V)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{*}\in\ell^{\infty}(V)</annotation></semantics></math> as <math id="S4.SS1.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\uparrow T_{\max}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">↑</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>max</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\uparrow T_{\max}</annotation></semantics></math>. Applying the Banach-space
Picard–Lindelöf theorem at time <math id="S4.SS1.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{\max}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{\max}</annotation></semantics></math> with initial value <math id="S4.SS1.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>u</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{*}</annotation></semantics></math> produces a local
solution beyond <math id="S4.SS1.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{\max}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{\max}</annotation></semantics></math>. Continuity of <math id="S4.SS1.p6.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}</annotation></semantics></math> and uniqueness glue it to the existing
solution, contradicting maximality. Therefore <math id="S4.SS1.p6.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{\max}=\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{\max}=\infty</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p7.1" class="ltx_p">Two exponential comparisons replace the formal chain rule that, in a diffusion setting, would identify <math id="S4.SS1.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S4.SS1.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\log(P_{t}\mathrm{e}^{u_{0}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\log(P_{t}\mathrm{e}^{u_{0}})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 4.2</span></span><span id="S4.Thmtheorem2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Scalar inequalities)<span id="S4.Thmtheorem2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For every <math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z\in[-1,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z\in[-1,1]</annotation></semantics></math>,</span></p>
<table id="S4.EGx6" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.E37"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(37)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E37.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\frac{1}{2}z\left(1+\frac{z}{2}\right)-\bigl(\mathrm{e}^{z/2}-1\bigr)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>z</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mi>z</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\frac{1}{2}z\left(1+\frac{z}{2}\right)-\bigl(\mathrm{e}^{z/2}-1\bigr)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E37.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\geq 0," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\geq 0,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.E38"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(38)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E38.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle 2z\left(1+\frac{z}{2}\right)-\bigl(\mathrm{e}^{2z}-1\bigr)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>z</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mi>z</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle 2z\left(1+\frac{z}{2}\right)-\bigl(\mathrm{e}^{2z}-1\bigr)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E38.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\leq 0." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\leq 0.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
