<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.p8.5" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p8.5.1" class="ltx_text">Using <math id="S5.p8.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C-Q_{0}\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C-Q_{0}\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math>, we obtain</span></p>
<table id="S5.E77" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(77)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.E77.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{x}(2r)=V_{x}(R_{0})\leq K_{\mathrm{heat}}V_{x}(r),\qquad K_{\mathrm{heat}}:=\frac{\mathrm{e}^{\beta Q_{0}}}{1-\mathrm{e}^{-\beta}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>heat</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>heat</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><mfrac><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></msup><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{x}(2r)=V_{x}(R_{0})\leq K_{\mathrm{heat}}V_{x}(r),\qquad K_{\mathrm{heat}}:=\frac{\mathrm{e}^{\beta Q_{0}}}{1-\mathrm{e}^{-\beta}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.p8.6" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p8.6.1" class="ltx_text">This holds uniformly for every integer <math id="S5.p8.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r\geq r_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>≥</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r\geq r_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p9.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p9.1.1" class="ltx_text">For <math id="S5.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\leq r&lt;r_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\leq r&lt;r_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>, the number of vertices at distance <math id="S5.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> from <math id="S5.p9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> is at most <math id="S5.p9.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{*}^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>d</mi><mo>∗</mo><mi>k</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{*}^{k}</annotation></semantics></math>. Since <math id="S5.p9.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B(x,2r)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B(x,2r)</annotation></semantics></math> contains only vertices at integer distance less than <math id="S5.p9.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2r</annotation></semantics></math>, the deliberately coarse degree estimate gives</span></p>
<table id="S5.E78" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(78)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.E78.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{x}(2r)\leq\sum_{k=0}^{2r_{0}}d_{*}^{k}\leq d_{*}^{2r_{0}+1}=:K_{\mathrm{loc}}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">≤</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></munderover><msubsup><mi>d</mi><mo>∗</mo><mi>k</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo>≤</mo><msubsup><mi>d</mi><mo>∗</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>loc</mi></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{x}(2r)\leq\sum_{k=0}^{2r_{0}}d_{*}^{k}\leq d_{*}^{2r_{0}+1}=:K_{\mathrm{loc}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.p9.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p9.2.1" class="ltx_text">while <math id="S5.p9.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{x}(r)\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{x}(r)\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math>. Combining the elementary and nontrivial degree cases, a valid global doubling constant is</span></p>
<table id="S5.E79" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(79)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.E79.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K(d_{*}):=\begin{cases}2,&amp;d_{*}\leq 1,\\[2.84526pt]
\displaystyle\max\left\{d_{*}^{2r_{0}+1},\frac{\mathrm{e}^{2Q_{0}}}{1-\mathrm{e}^{-2}}\right\},&amp;d_{*}\geq 2,\end{cases}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo>≤</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><msubsup><mi>d</mi><mo>∗</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mfrac><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></msup><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mfrac><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K(d_{*}):=\begin{cases}2,&amp;d_{*}\leq 1,\\[2.84526pt]
\displaystyle\max\left\{d_{*}^{2r_{0}+1},\frac{\mathrm{e}^{2Q_{0}}}{1-\mathrm{e}^{-2}}\right\},&amp;d_{*}\geq 2,\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.p9.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p9.3.1" class="ltx_text">where, in the second branch, <math id="S5.p9.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N,\ell_{*},C,Q_{0},r_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>C</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N,\ell_{*},C,Q_{0},r_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> are given by (<a href="#S2.E1" title="In 2. Preliminary ‣ Nonnegative Bakry–Émery Curvature on Bounded-Degree Graphs Implies Volume Doubling and Poincaré Inequalities" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>), (<a href="#S5.E61" title="In 5. Volume doubling and polynomial growth ‣ Nonnegative Bakry–Émery Curvature on Bounded-Degree Graphs Implies Volume Doubling and Poincaré Inequalities" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">61</span></a>), (<a href="#S5.E62" title="In 5. Volume doubling and polynomial growth ‣ Nonnegative Bakry–Émery Curvature on Bounded-Degree Graphs Implies Volume Doubling and Poincaré Inequalities" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">62</span></a>), and (<a href="#S5.E64" title="In 5. Volume doubling and polynomial growth ‣ Nonnegative Bakry–Émery Curvature on Bounded-Degree Graphs Implies Volume Doubling and Poincaré Inequalities" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">64</span></a>). This proves Theorem <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 1.1. ‣ 1.2. The volume-growth problem and its history ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Nonnegative Bakry–Émery Curvature on Bounded-Degree Graphs Implies Volume Doubling and Poincaré Inequalities" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.1</span></a>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p10.1" class="ltx_p">The corollary is the direct dyadic iteration of the theorem.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.4" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S5.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p11.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p11.1.1" class="ltx_text">For Corollary <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem2" title="Corollary 1.2. ‣ 1.2. The volume-growth problem and its history ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Nonnegative Bakry–Émery Curvature on Bounded-Degree Graphs Implies Volume Doubling and Poincaré Inequalities" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.2</span></a>, because the balls are open,</span></p>
<table id="S5.Ex101" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex101.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B(x,1)=\{x\}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B(x,1)=\{x\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.p11.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p11.2.1" class="ltx_text">Iterating Theorem <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 1.1. ‣ 1.2. The volume-growth problem and its history ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Nonnegative Bakry–Émery Curvature on Bounded-Degree Graphs Implies Volume Doubling and Poincaré Inequalities" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.1</span></a> gives</span></p>
<table id="S5.Ex102" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex102.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\#B(x,2^{k})\leq K(d_{*})^{k}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>#</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>k</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\#B(x,2^{k})\leq K(d_{*})^{k}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.p11.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p11.3.1" class="ltx_text">Let <math id="S5.p11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math> be an integer and put</span></p>
<table id="S5.Ex103" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex103.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k:=\lceil\log_{2}r\rceil." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>log</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌉</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k:=\lceil\log_{2}r\rceil.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.p11.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p11.4.1" class="ltx_text">Then</span></p>
<table id="S5.EGx15" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S5.Ex104"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.Ex104.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\#B(x,r)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>#</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\#B(x,r)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.Ex104.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\leq K(d_{*})^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\leq K(d_{*})^{k}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S5.Ex105"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.Ex105.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\leq K(d_{*})r^{\log_{2}K(d_{*})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>r</mi><mrow><msub><mi>log</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\leq K(d_{*})r^{\log_{2}K(d_{*})}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S5.Ex106"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.Ex106.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\leq r^{2\log_{2}K(d_{*})}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><msup><mi>r</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msub><mi>log</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\leq r^{2\log_{2}K(d_{*})}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.p11.5" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p11.5.1" class="ltx_text">The last inequality uses <math id="S5.p11.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.p11.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K(d_{*})\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K(d_{*})\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math>. The case <math id="S5.p11.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r=1</annotation></semantics></math> is an equality. Taking
the ceiling of the exponent gives the stated value of <math id="S5.p11.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D(d_{*})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D(d_{*})</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">6. </span>Heat-kernel localisation for Poincaré inequalities</h2>

<div id="S6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This section proves the local analytic estimate needed for Theorem <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem3" title="Theorem 1.3. ‣ 1.3. The local Poincaré problem ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Nonnegative Bakry–Émery Curvature on Bounded-Degree Graphs Implies Volume Doubling and Poincaré Inequalities" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.3</span></a>.
First, reverse Poincaré smoothing controls the total-variation distance between nearby
heat-kernel rows. The semigroup estimate from Subsection <a href="#S3.SS2" title="3.2. Resolvent smoothing ‣ 3. Point-mass estimates and resolvent smoothing ‣ Nonnegative Bakry–Émery Curvature on Bounded-Degree Graphs Implies Volume Doubling and Poincaré Inequalities" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a> gives a
uniform first-moment displacement bound for the associated walk, and a standard
strong-Markov maximal argument converts it into an exit-time estimate. We then compare the
full walk with the finite-state chain obtained by rejecting jumps that leave a large ball. A
finite-dimensional localisation lemma converts the resulting row overlap into a spectral-gap
estimate. Spectral calculus yields a scale-invariant Poincaré inequality with a fixed
degree-dependent dilation. Volume doubling is not used in this section.</p>
</div>
<section id="S6.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">6.1. </span>Heat-kernel row contraction and exit-time control</h3>

