<section id="S7" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">7. </span>Reduction to dilation two</h2>

<div id="S7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In standard geodesic doubling metric-measure formulations, a weak Poincaré inequality
with any fixed enlargement can be upgraded to the strong form by a Whitney-covering
argument going back to Jerison; see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite>, the detailed account in
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">37</a>, Sections 5.3.2–5.3.5]</cite>, and the metric-measure/random-walk formulation in
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">32</a>, Chapter 3 in the arXiv version]</cite>. Our inequality is stated for open integer graph
balls and uses the induced edge energy, and we also need to retain quantitative dependence
only on <math id="S7.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{*}</annotation></semantics></math>. For completeness, we therefore include a discrete covering-and-chaining
argument adapted to these conventions, yielding the conventional dilation two.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The volume-doubling theorem bounds the number of balls in a separated covering. Local
Poincaré inequalities control oscillation inside each ball, and a connected graph of centres
controls the differences of their local means. Small radii are handled directly by geodesics.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S7.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text">If <math id="S7.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{*}\leq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo>≤</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{*}\leq 1</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="S7.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> has at most two vertices. For <math id="S7.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r=1</annotation></semantics></math>, the left-hand side is zero. For
<math id="S7.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math>, one has <math id="S7.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B(o,r)=B(o,2r)=V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>o</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>o</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B(o,r)=B(o,2r)=V</annotation></semantics></math>. The one-vertex case is trivial, and on the two-vertex
graph</span></p>
<table id="S7.Ex169" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.Ex169.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{x\in V}|f(x)-f_{V}|^{2}=\frac{1}{2}\mathcal{E}_{V}(f)\leq r^{2}\mathcal{E}_{V}(f)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></munder><msup><mrow><mo lspace="0em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>V</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>V</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msup><mi>r</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>V</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{x\in V}|f(x)-f_{V}|^{2}=\frac{1}{2}\mathcal{E}_{V}(f)\leq r^{2}\mathcal{E}_{V}(f).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.p3.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.p3.2.1" class="ltx_text">Thus <math id="S7.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{P}(d_{*})=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>P</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{P}(d_{*})=1</annotation></semantics></math> suffices when <math id="S7.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{*}\leq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo>≤</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{*}\leq 1</annotation></semantics></math>. Assume henceforth that <math id="S7.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{*}\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{*}\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math>. Put</span></p>
